data: {"run_id":"c50ede4d9d30468d9f715aa33168273e","seq":10,"stage":"respond","status":"ok","parent":null,"label":"10","data":{"action":"answer","system":"You are a career agent inside a job-search product. Answer the user's career question helpfully and concretely. Keep the answer under 200 words.","max_tokens":300,"max_chars":1500,"provider":"anthropic","model":"claude-haiku-4-5","text":"# How to Negotiate Your Salary Offer\n\n**Do Your Research**\n- Use Glassdoor, PayScale, or LinkedIn Salary to find typical pay for your role, location, and experience level\n- Know your market value before discussing numbers\n\n**Timing Matters**\n- Negotiate *after* receiving a formal offer, not during initial conversations\n- Don't mention salary first—let them anchor if possible\n\n**Make Your Case**\n- Focus on value: your skills, achievements, and what you'll contribute\n- Reference market data: \"Based on industry standards for this role...\"\n- Mention competing offers if relevant (honestly)\n\n**Negotiation Tips**\n- Start 10-15% above their offer (leave room to meet in middle)\n- Ask for the salary range first, rather than stating a number\n- Consider negotiating benefits too: PTO, remote work, signing bonus, professional development\n\n**Stay Professional**\n- Be confident but not aggressive\n- Express genuine enthusiasm for the role\n- Avoid ultimatums\n- Get any final offer in writing\n\n**Know When to Accept**\n- You don't have to negotiate aggressively if the offer meets your needs\n- A reasonable offer + growth opportunity might be better than maximum salary at wrong company\n\nRemember: most employers expect negotiation. A polite, well-reasoned ask rarely hurts your candidacy.","truncated":false,"recorded_at":"2026-10-29T14:33:37+00:00","replayed":true},"ms":4003,"tokens":349,"cost_usd":0.001549}

